<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Curriculum sub-strand: Essential 2.4 -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-commercial-arithmetic-iii" component="essential" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Commercial Arithmetic III</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Money changes in value over time. Savings in a bank or SACCO earn interest, a loan costs more than the amount borrowed, land usually gains value, and a motorbike loses value as it is used. You have already met percentages, profit and loss, and simple interest.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this chapter you will calculate simple interest and then compound interest using the formula. You will work out the appreciation and depreciation of assets, compare the hire purchase cost of goods with the cash price, and use the tax table to compute the income tax and net income of an employee.
    </p>
    <p>
      These are skills you will use throughout your life: choosing where to save, deciding whether to borrow or to buy on hire purchase, and understanding your payslip.
    </p>
  </introduction>

